# Hermes Sentinel — Stage C3 reporter environment file EXAMPLE.
#
# This is a SAFE SYNTHETIC TEMPLATE. An operator copies it to
# /etc/hermes-sentinel/reporter.env (root:root, 0600) and edits it
# locally on the monitored host (see docs/REPORTER_DEPLOYMENT.md).
#
# This is a systemd EnvironmentFile, NOT a shell profile: values are
# plain KEY=value pairs read by the systemd manager, which supplies
# them to the reporter service process. Never write `export ...` here,
# never quote values unnecessarily, and never pass the token on a
# command line (it would land in shell history and process listings).
#
# Exactly three configuration variables exist — nothing else:
#
#   SENTINEL_NODE      explicit node identity (never inferred from the
#                      hostname). Node names such as Prod, Hermes,
#                      VPN-1, VPN-2 are configured explicitly here and
#                      stay verbatim/case-sensitive.
#   SENTINEL_ENDPOINT  the FULL HTTPS heartbeat endpoint URL, e.g.
#                      https://sentinel.example/v1/heartbeat — the
#                      reporter uses it verbatim (HTTPS only).
#   SENTINEL_TOKEN     the per-node reporter token issued for this
#                      node by the Sentinel operator.
#
# The example values below are deliberately synthetic:
#   - the endpoint uses the reserved `.invalid` TLD (RFC 2606) and can
#     never resolve to a real server;
#   - the token placeholder `<REPLACE_ME>` is deliberately INVALID
#     under the reporter token validation, so an accidentally unedited
#     example fails closed BEFORE any network delivery.
# No real token, server, domain or IP belongs in this file.

SENTINEL_NODE=REPLACE_ME
SENTINEL_ENDPOINT=https://sentinel.invalid/v1/heartbeat
SENTINEL_TOKEN=<REPLACE_ME>
